A||N||2||merlin||6:00 / 11:58 Timing chain||||5||
Z||000000||merlin||06-21-2001||08:51 PM||||I own a 1987 V6 GT and I’m in the process of replacing the timing chain and gears.  Prior to removing the timing chain I made sure that the camshaft gear and the crankshaft gear were at the 6:00 and 12:00 position respectively.  However, after I removed the timing chain I ended up moving the crankshaft gear about two inches when I was using the puller.  I installed the new gears and chain and corrected the crankshaft gear to the approximate 12 o’clock position.  I say ‘approximate’ because after about an hour of fiddling with the chain and gears, the closest looks like it’s about a 1/2 a tooth off on the crankshaft.  Maybe I’m just paranoid, but is it obvious when the two timing marks are out of sync, such as it always being off a whole tooth rather than a half?  Does anyone have any tips or a double-check that I could do.  It hasn’t been a fun time with this project and I don’t want to have to repeat it.    <P>Thanks<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||06-21-2001||09:21 PM||||Pull the number one spark plug and see if the number one cylinder is at TDC. If it's close than just move the crankshaft until the mark on the gear is @ approximately the 12:00 position. Then line the cam mark up with the mark on the crankshaft using a straight edge so that the straight edge is in line with the center of the gears and marks. The marks should all be inline with the center of the crankshaft and the camshaft gear and line up with the marks on the cam and crankshaft gears.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
